---
id: dill-lemon-baked-salmon
title: Dill & Lemon Baked Salmon
category: modular_protein

yield:
  servings: 2
  cooked_g: 260

storage:
  fridge_days: 2
  freezer_friendly: false

---

Set the #oven to convection 220°C (425°F) and let it preheat fully, about 10 to 15 minutes.

Pat @atlantic-salmon-steaks{1%package} (2 steaks, about 2.5 cm / 1 inch thick) completely dry with paper towels.

Stir together @olive-oil{1%tsp} (salmon brings its own fat; this just helps the herbs cling), @fresh-garlic{2%clove} finely grated, the finely grated zest and juice of @fresh-lemon{1/2}, @fresh-dill{10%g} finely chopped, @kosher-salt{1/4%tsp}, and @black-pepper{1/4%tsp}.

Lay both steaks flat on the mesh basket of the #crisper-tray and spread the mixture over the tops.

Roast on the middle rack for ~roast{10%minute}, checking from the 8-minute mark. Salmon goes from moist to dry in about 2 minutes, so let the thermometer decide: pull the steaks when the thickest part, next to the bone, reads 52°C to 54°C (126°F–129°F) on a #meat-thermometer. They climb a couple of degrees as they rest and flake under gentle fork pressure. Pulling them slightly under also leaves room for the second steak to be warmed up later without drying out.

Rest for ~rest{2%minute}. Eat one steak now. Lift out the centre bone and any loose small bones with a fork as you eat; they come away easily once cooked.

Refrigerate the second steak in a container and eat it the next day, either cold or at room temperature (salmon is good cold, flaked over a salad or the sweet potatoes), or gently warmed: cover it with a damp paper towel and microwave at 50% power for 60 to 90 seconds. Full power dries it out and makes it smell fishy.
